WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.830 --> 00:00:06.109
Everybody knows Hollywood loves remaking classic
movies, but that's not always a bad thing.

00:00:06.109 --> 00:00:11.110
Sure, there are plenty of boring copycats
out there, but for every unimaginative reimagining,

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:15.440
there's an amazing remake that feels nothing
like the original.

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:16.520
The Thing

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:22.460
John Carpenter's 1982 film The Thing is a
remake of the 1951 The Thing from Another

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:23.460
World.

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:26.199
But as far as remakes go, the two movies are
different creatures.

00:00:26.199 --> 00:00:32.840
Sure, both versions are set in snowy wastelands,
where an alien terrorizes humans and murders

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:33.840
sled dogs.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:38.790
In both movies, the Thing hates fire and is
defeated by a pilot, but that's where the

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:39.980
similarities end.

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:44.540
In the '51 movie, the monster is a gigantic
humanoid vegetable.

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:49.320
The humans work together to defeat it, which
is the opposite of what happens in the remake.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:54.320
Instead of fighting an intellectual carrot,
the characters in the '82 film face a slimy

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:56.900
shape-shifter that absorbs and impersonates
its victims.

00:00:56.900 --> 00:01:03.710
The heroes in The Thing are constantly suspicious
of each other, and never sure who's human.

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:09.330
Most importantly, the '51 film ends with the
alien defeated, but the '82 film has one of

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:14.780
the most ambiguous endings ever, as bleak
and cold as Outpost 31.

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:23.900
"Why don't we just wait here for a little
while… and see what happens"

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:25.100
Pale Rider

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:28.390
Shane is one of the all-time great Westerns.

00:01:28.390 --> 00:01:34.409
This 1953 film follows a mysterious gunfighter
played by Alan Ladd, who defends homesteaders

00:01:34.409 --> 00:01:36.450
from an evil cattle baron.

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:40.800
In 1985, Clint Eastwood unofficially remade
the movie as Pale Rider.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:46.159
As the enigmatic Preacher, Eastwood rides
into a downtrodden mining camp and defends

00:01:46.159 --> 00:01:48.810
the locals from a powerful gold tycoon.

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:54.350
In both movies, a cold-blooded gunslinger
standing on a porch shoots down a well-meaning

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:55.350
hothead.

00:01:55.350 --> 00:02:00.280
And both movies end with the hero riding into
the distance, with an obsessed kid calling

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:01.530
out after him.

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:05.729
Despite the similarities, there's one enormous,
otherworldly difference.

00:02:05.729 --> 00:02:11.220
Shane is human, and Preacher is the ghost
of a dead gunfighter, who only shows up after

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:13.410
a little girl asks God for a miracle.

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:14.500
"And behold a pale horse … and his name
that sat on him was Death.

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:15.500
And hell followed with him."

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:16.500
Little Shop of Horrors

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:21.980
The 1986 film Little Shop of Horrors is a
big, brassy musical about a nerdy florist,

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:26.319
played by Rick Moranis, and a man-eating plant
with dreams of world dominance.

00:02:26.319 --> 00:02:31.760
Directed by Frank Oz, this over-the-top monster
flick features Steve Martin as a sadistic

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:34.420
dentist and a giant singing Venus flytrap.

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:39.810
The film was inspired by an off-Broadway play,
but if you go further back, you can follow

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:42.440
Little Shop's roots to the 1960 original.

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:47.280
While the basic plot is the same for both
movies, the original wasn't a musical.

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:51.630
The blood-hungry plant is far more talkative
in the remake, but the biggest difference

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:52.690
is the ending.

00:02:52.690 --> 00:02:57.010
In the remake's theatrical ending, the hero
saves the world from the giant plant, but

00:02:57.010 --> 00:03:01.690
in the original, the lead is devoured and
turned into the world's most horrific flower.

00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:05.060
"He was such a good boy …"

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:07.960
"Seymour!"

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:10.700
"I didn't mean it"

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:12.260
12 Monkeys

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:17.430
In 1995's 12 Monkeys, a super virus has ravaged
the Earth.

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:22.319
The survivors send a prisoner, played by Bruce
Willis, back in time to find the disease's

00:03:22.319 --> 00:03:23.319
origin.

00:03:23.319 --> 00:03:27.820
While it certainly feels like an original
movie, its basic premise was lifted from a

00:03:27.820 --> 00:03:33.560
1962 French film called La Jetée, which shares
a nearly identical plot.

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:38.450
Just like the prisoner in 12 Monkeys, the
hero of La Jetée is haunted by a childhood

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:41.450
memory of a man gunned down at an airport.

00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:45.989
In both films, the hero discovers that, thanks
to the curse of time travel, the guy he saw

00:03:45.989 --> 00:03:51.340
shot down at an airport was his future self,
and now he's stuck in a never-ending time

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:52.340
loop.

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:56.830
What truly sets these movies apart is that
12 Monkeys is a traditional movie while La

00:03:56.830 --> 00:03:59.070
Jetée is experimental art.

00:03:59.070 --> 00:04:03.709
The black-and-white French film is only 28
minutes long and is told with still images

00:04:03.709 --> 00:04:04.910
and a narrator.

00:04:04.910 --> 00:04:06.940
Rise of the Planet of the Apes

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:12.019
The modern Planet of the Apes franchise takes
a whole lot of liberties from the original

00:04:12.019 --> 00:04:17.909
Ape pentalogy, so you might not realize that
2011's Rise of the Planet of the Apes is also

00:04:17.909 --> 00:04:23.999
a remake of the fourth film in the original
series: 1972's Conquest of the Planet of the

00:04:23.999 --> 00:04:24.999
Apes.

00:04:24.999 --> 00:04:30.020
Both movies follow an ape named Caesar who
leads a primate revolt against humans.

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:35.970
But the Caesar of the 2011 remake, played
by Andy Serkis, is far more benevolent than

00:04:35.970 --> 00:04:39.310
the original Caesar, played by Roddy McDowall.

00:04:39.310 --> 00:04:44.289
Serkis' character is a Moses-type who leads
his fellow apes to freedom, while McDowall's

00:04:44.289 --> 00:04:47.870
Caesar is driven by righteous anger and vengeance.

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:53.930
Rise takes place in modern times, whereas
the 1972 Conquest takes place in a totalitarian

00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:56.860
era where apes are used for slave labor.

00:04:56.860 --> 00:05:01.520
But perhaps the most important distinction
is that in Rise, the apes use spears, and

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:04.840
in Conquest, they kill bad guys with machine
guns.

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:08.520
The moden reboot would have to wait another
movie for that.

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:09.520
Pete's Dragon

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:15.879
If you compare the 1977 Pete's Dragon to the
2016 remake, you could sum up the similarities

00:05:15.879 --> 00:05:21.159
in one sentence: "An orphan boy named Pete
befriends a magical dragon named Elliott."

00:05:21.159 --> 00:05:24.599
Other than that, these two movies are totally
different beasts.

00:05:24.599 --> 00:05:29.779
Most notably, the original Elliott is covered
in scales, but the new one looks like a fuzzy

00:05:29.779 --> 00:05:30.900
green dog.

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:35.490
And Pete's backstory is different; In the
original, he's a runaway, and in the remake,

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:38.610
he's been stranded in the forest after a car
crash.

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:42.689
Both films feature a villain who wants to
capture the dragon, although the motives of

00:05:42.689 --> 00:05:45.400
the '77 villain are somewhat more sinister.

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:49.379
"We could make a million by slicing him, dicing
him..."

00:05:49.379 --> 00:05:53.839
The biggest difference between the two films
is that the original is a musical, while there's

00:05:53.839 --> 00:05:56.529
not a single show tune in the remake.

00:05:56.529 --> 00:06:00.319
That's probably a good thing because we're
not sure if Robert Redford could have pulled

00:06:00.319 --> 00:06:03.589
off the whole Mickey Rooney song-and-dance
routine.

00:06:03.589 --> 00:06:04.589
The Magnificent Seven

00:06:04.589 --> 00:06:12.419
Antoine Fuqua's 2016 remake of The Magnificent
Seven features a mustachioed Denzel Washington

00:06:12.419 --> 00:06:16.770
and a cowboy Chris Pratt fighting an army
of gunslinging assassins.

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:22.520
It might not compare to the 1960 version,
which was itself a remake of Seven Samurai,

00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:25.619
but the modern update got some much-needed
upgrades.

00:06:25.619 --> 00:06:30.439
The cast is much more diverse, and one of
the most important characters is female, which

00:06:30.439 --> 00:06:32.830
is allows for some more dynamic storytelling.

00:06:32.830 --> 00:06:38.419
In the original, Mexican farmers fend off
bandits, but in the remake, a group of townsfolk

00:06:38.419 --> 00:06:41.699
defend their property from a psychopathic
robber baron.

00:06:41.699 --> 00:06:46.529
Washington's character is partly driven by
revenge, while Yul Brynner's man in black

00:06:46.529 --> 00:06:50.169
is fighting for the farmers solely because
it's the right thing to do.

00:06:50.169 --> 00:06:53.980
Both movies have their pros and cons, but
at the end of the day, well …

00:06:55.180 --> 00:06:59.320
"It was magnificent."

